see "../src/harness.vox".

(A program that exits cleanly. Every fixture in this test reaches its
 fate through /bin/sh BUILT-INS only - true, sleep, and friends do not
 exist inside the Nix build sandbox, and this test must pass there too.)
a list called 'the clean arguments' is ["-c", "exit 0"].
a text called 'how true ended' is supervise of "/bin/sh" and 'the clean arguments' and 5000.
Print "true: {'how true ended'} {'exit code of' of 'the last status'}".

(A program that exits with a code above 128 - the case a shell-reported
 exit code could not tell apart from a signal death.)
a list called 'the exit arguments' is ["-c", "exit 200"].
a text called 'how the exit ended' is supervise of "/bin/sh" and 'the exit arguments' and 5000.
Print "exit 200: {'how the exit ended'} {'exit code of' of 'the last status'}".

(A program killed by a signal it did not choose.)
a list called 'the segfault arguments' is ["-c", "kill -SEGV $$"].
a text called 'how it died' is supervise of "/bin/sh" and 'the segfault arguments' and 5000.
Print "segv: {'how it died'} {'signal of' of 'the last status'} {crashed of 'the last status'}".

(A program that never finishes, ended by the harness itself. A busy
 loop of shell built-ins, because the sandbox has no sleep binary; the
 300ms deadline keeps the burn short.)
a list called 'the spin arguments' is ["-c", "while :; do :; done"].
a text called 'how the spin ended' is supervise of "/bin/sh" and 'the spin arguments' and 300.
Print "sleep: {'how the spin ended'} {'signal of' of 'the last status'}".